WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.332
Hi, I'm Peanut!

00:00:02.332 --> 00:00:05.804
Jev-Omni picks an option from text, images, audio or video.

00:00:05.804 --> 00:00:08.582
Here's how all four reach one small head.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:13.981
Pictures and sound become tokens through one small linear layer each.

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:17.820
One decoder reads everything, and a two hundred and fifty-six way

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.216
head picks the answer.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:24.298
Gemma 4 has no vision tower and no audio encoder.

00:00:24.298 --> 00:00:28.097
A flat square of pixels, or a slice of raw samples, goes

00:00:28.097 --> 00:00:29.679
through a single linear layer.

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:34.280
Those tokens join the text prompt, options numbered, in one decoder.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.080
The decoder was fine-tuned; the front end is Google's, untouched.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.689
A head reads the last position: one linear layer to two hundred

00:00:42.689 --> 00:00:45.831
and fifty-six classes, with the unused classes masked away.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.347
That whole front end is fifty-two million parameters, zero point four four

00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:52.696
percent of the decoder.

00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:55.394
The four modalities come with the base model.

00:00:56.983 --> 00:01:00.301
A per-option scorer rates each option on its own, so the list

00:01:00.301 --> 00:01:02.512
is just data and the order can't matter.

00:01:02.932 --> 00:01:06.595
Here, class one is whatever comes first in the list, and the

00:01:06.595 --> 00:01:08.427
count is welded into the weights.

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:09.648
So order can matter.

00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:11.175
That's reasoned, not yet tested.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:17.401
The card's own numbers: text, image and audio all land under a hundred milliseconds.

00:01:17.401 --> 00:01:20.573
Sixteen frames of video take five hundred and four.

00:01:22.142 --> 00:01:26.001
Four modalities reach one head because the base model already turns

00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:27.405
them all into tokens.

00:01:27.405 --> 00:01:29.510
The decision training was text training.

00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:35.394
To recap: tiny linear front ends, one fine-tuned decoder, and the option

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:37.146
count in the head's weights.

00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:39.623
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:40.211
I'm Peanut.

00:01:40.211 --> 00:01:40.505
Bye!
